// A negative byte, and it is a SEPARATE fixture from the one above rather than a second line in it,
// because it reaches the rule down a different path in the AST.
//
// **`-1` is a unary minus WRAPPING the literal `1`** — the lexer reads no sign, so there is no
// literal node holding -1 anywhere. A range check that only looked at an integer literal would
// therefore see nothing at all for every negative argument, and would pass this file by building it.
//
// That is not hypothetical. `c_bytes_at`'s negative-count rule in the Burxt compiler was written that
// way and never fired once for as long as it existed; a fixture found it, not a reading. Both
// compilers now unwrap the minus in one shared place, and this file is what stops it silently
// un-firing again.
print(byte_as_string(-1));
